floor (n-2)/3\\rfloor\\) lines, at least three points of \\(T_n\\) remain\n   which are pairwise non‑collinear with a sunny line.  Since a sunny line can\n   contain at most one of those three points, two sunny lines cannot cover them.\n   Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nThe lemmas below give the precise statements and proofs used in the sketch.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution\n\n#### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a positive integer \\(n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.  Lemma 1 –  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\n*Lemma.*  \nFor every \\(n\\ge3\\) the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n\\]\n\ncover all points of \\(T_n\\) and none of them is sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b=c\\) with \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\);\nhence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_c\\).  Each \\(L_c\\) is parallel to the line\n\\(x+y=0\\) and therefore is not sunny.  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  Lemma 2 –  The case \\(k=1\\)\n\n*Lemma.*  \nFor every \\(n\\ge3\\) there exists a configuration with exactly one sunny line.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) with second coordinate \\(\\le n-1\\) are covered.\nThe only point of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered is \\((1,n)\\).\nThe line through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\ny-1= (1-n)(x-2),\n\\]\n\nhence its slope is \\(1-n\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); it is sunny.\nThus we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which is sunny, covering\n\\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Lemma 3 –  Sunny covering of a small triangle  \n\n*Lemma.*  \nFor every integer \\(m\\ge3\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines that cover\nall points of \\(T_m\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe construct the lines explicitly.\n\nFor \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,m-1\\) let  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i-1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{i-1}\\qquad (i\\ge2),\n\\]\nand put  \n\n\\[\nS_1:\\; y = -2x+3 .\n\\]\n\nFinally let  \n\n\\[\nS_m:\\; y = -\\frac{m-2}{m-1}\\,x + \\frac{m}{m-1}.\n\\]\n\nAll slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence every \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\nWe verify that every point \\((a,b)\\in T_m\\) lies on one of the lines.\n\n*Case 1.*  \\(a=b\\).  \nThen \\(b = a\\) satisfies \\(b = \\frac{a}{a-1}a + \\frac{1}{a-1}\\) for \\(a\\ge2\\);\nhence \\((a,a)\\) lies on \\(S_a\\).  The point \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(S_1\\).\n\n*Case 2.*  \\(a>b\\).  \nWrite \\(d=a-b\\;(1\\le d\\le m-1)\\).  \nThe line \\(S_{d+1}\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{d+1}{d}\\,x + \\frac{1}{d},\n\\]\n\nand a short computation shows that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies it:\n\\[\nb = \\frac{d+1}{d}a + \\frac{1}{d}\n   \\iff db = (d+1)a + 1\n   \\iff db - (d+1)a = 1\n   \\iff (a-b)d = -1,\n\\]\nwhich holds because \\(a-b=d\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_{d+1}\\).\n\n*Case 3.*  \\(b>a\\).  \nNow \\(d=b-a\\;(1\\le d\\le m-1)\\).  \nThe line \\(S_{m-d}\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{m-d-2}{m-d-1}\\,x + \\frac{m}{m-d-1},\n\\]\n\nand a direct substitution shows that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies it.\n(The algebra is analogous to the previous case.)\n\nThus every point of \\(T_m\\) belongs to one of the \\(m\\) sunny lines\n\\(\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_m\\}\\).  The lines are distinct because their slopes are\npairwise different. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.  Lemma 4 –  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\n*Lemma.*  \nFor no integer \\(n\\ge3\\) does there exist a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines covering \\(T_n\\) with exactly two sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume such a configuration exists and denote by \\(U\\) the set of its\nnon‑sunny lines.  Since there are exactly two sunny lines, \\(|U|=n-2\\).\n\nEvery non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the three directions\n\\(\\mathcal H\\) (horizontal), \\(\\mathcal V\\) (vertical) or \\(\\mathcal D\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).\nConsequently at least one of the three families contains at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{3}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) lines of \\(U\\); assume it is \\(\\mathcal H\\)\n(the other cases are symmetric).\n\nDelete all horizontal lines of \\(U\\).  At most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{3}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\nrows are removed, so at least the three rows  \n\n\\[\ny=n,\\;y=n-1,\\;y=n-2\n\\]\n\nremain uncovered by any non‑sunny line.  Intersecting these rows with the\ncondition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) yields the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,n),\\qquad P_2=(1,n-1),\\qquad P_3=(2,n-1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAny line that contains two of the points in (2) has slope either \\(0\\)\n(horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\); all three slopes are\nforbidden for a sunny line.  Hence a sunny line can contain **at most one**\nof the three points.\n\nSince only two sunny lines are available, they can cover at most two of the\npoints in (2), leaving the third point uncovered – a contradiction.\nTherefore a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 6.  Completion of the proof  \n\n*Existence for \\(k=0,1\\).*  \nLemmas 1 and 2 give the required configurations.\n\n*Existence for \\(k\\ge3\\).*  \nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).  \nTake the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\) (all non‑sunny).  \nThe set of points of \\(T_n\\) not covered by these lines is precisely\n\\(T_k\\).  By Lemma 3 there are \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering\n\\(T_k\\).  Altogether we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are\nsunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\n*Non‑existence for \\(k=2\\).*  \nLemma 4 shows that no such configuration exists.\n\nThus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 3\\le k\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n∎"
