\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\}\\) can be covered by \\(m\\) sunny lines for \\(m\\neq2\\) is invalid.\n\n**b. Method Sketch (corrected solution):**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   - For a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) let  \n\n     \\[\n     T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n     \\]\n\n   - A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n2. **Key Lemma (corrected).**  \n   For every integer \\(m\\ge3\\) the set \\(T_m\\) can be covered by exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines.  \n   *Proof idea.*  We partition the points of \\(T_m\\) into \\(m\\) disjoint pairs (and, when \\(m\\) is odd, one single point).  Each pair is chosen so that the line through the two points has a slope belonging to \\(\\mathbb Q\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\\).  Because the pairs are disjoint, the \\(m\\) lines are distinct and all are sunny.  An explicit description of the pairing is given below; a point‑by‑point verification shows that every point of \\(T_m\\) belongs to one of the lines.\n\n3. **Construction for the original problem (all admissible \\(k\\)).**  \n   - Let \\(k\\) be a non‑negative integer with \\(k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq2\\).  \n   - Put \\(t=n-k\\).  Choose the \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines to be the vertical lines  \n\n     \\[\n     x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=t .\n     \\]\n\n     These lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(t\\).  \n   - The uncovered points are exactly  \n\n     \\[\n     U=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a\\ge t+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n       =\\{(a',b)\\mid a',b\\ge1,\\;a'+b\\le k+1\\}=T_k .\n     \\]\n\n   - By the Lemma, \\(T_k\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines (when \\(k=0\\) there is nothing to cover).  \n   - The union of the \\(t\\) vertical lines and the \\(k\\) sunny lines gives \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   Assume a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.  The remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are non‑sunny, i.e. each is vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).  Such a line can eliminate at most one *family* of “layers’’ of the triangle: a column, a row, or a \\((-1)\\)-diagonal.  Because there are \\(n\\) columns, \\(n\\) rows and \\(n-1\\) diagonals, after using at most \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines at least one of the three families still contains **two** distinct layers that are untouched.  Those two untouched layers contain a copy of the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\},\n   \\]\n\n   after a suitable translation.  By a direct check (see below) covering the three points of \\(T_2\\) requires **at least three** sunny lines; two sunny lines are insufficient.  This contradiction shows that \\(k=2\\) is impossible for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n5. **Explicit verification for \\(T_2\\).**  \n   The three points of \\(T_2\\) are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n   - Any line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) is vertical (slope ∞).  \n   - Any line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) is horizontal (slope 0).  \n   - Any line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) has slope \\(-1\\).  \n   Hence no single sunny line can contain two of the three points; at least three sunny lines are needed.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the non‑negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\) except \\(k=2\\).  In compact form  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n,\\;k\\neq2\\,\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\