"### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n,\\;k\\neq 2,\\;k\\neq 4\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *For a given integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) let*  \n\n   \\[\n   T_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *A line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).*\n\n2. **Reduction.**  \n   If we can cover \\(T_m\\) with exactly \\(m\\) sunny lines, then for any \\(n\\ge m\\) we obtain a configuration with  \n   * \\(k=m\\) sunny lines,  \n   * \\(n-m\\) additional non\u2011sunny vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-m\\).  \n   Hence the problem reduces to determining for which \\(m\\) the set \\(T_m\\) can be covered by exactly \\(m\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Impossibility for \\(m=2\\) and \\(m=4\\).**  \n   *\\(m=2\\):* \\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\). Any line containing two of these points has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore at least three sunny lines are needed.  \n   *\\(m=4\\):* \\(T_4\\) contains the whole first column \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le4\\}\\). Because a sunny line is never vertical, each sunny line can contain **at most one** point of this column. Consequently at least four sunny lines are required, and each of the four lines must contain exactly one column\u20111 point.  \n   The four points of the first row \\(\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le4\\}\\) must also be covered, so each of the four lines must contain a distinct row\u20111 point. The only possible pairings are \\((1,b_i)\\) with \\((a_i,1)\\) where \\(\\{b_i\\}=\\{a_i\\}=\\{1,2,3,4\\}\\). The pair \\((1,1)\\) gives a horizontal line (slope \\(0\\)), the pair \\((1,4)\\) with \\((4,1)\\) gives slope \\(-1\\); both are non\u2011sunny. Any other pairing forces a line to have slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\). Hence no collection of four sunny lines can cover all points of \\(T_4\\).  \n\n   Thus **\\(m=2\\) and \\(m=4\\) are impossible**.\n\n4. **Existence for all other \\(m\\).**  \n   We prove by induction on \\(m\\) that for every integer \\(m\\ge 1,\\;m\\neq2,4\\) the set \\(T_m\\) can be covered by exactly \\(m\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Base cases.*  \n\n   *\\(m=0\\):* empty set, no line needed.  \n   *\\(m=1\\):* the single point \\((1,1)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(y=x\\).  \n   *\\(m=3\\):* the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y = x      &&\\text{covers }(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\n   L_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52 &&\\text{covers }(1,2),(3,1),\\\\\n   L_3 &: y = -2x +5 &&\\text{covers }(1,3),(2,1)\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   together contain all six points of \\(T_3\\).  \n   *\\(m=5\\):* the five sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y = x,\\\\\n   L_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac72,\\\\\n   L_3 &: y = -2x +7,\\\\\n   L_4 &: y = -\\tfrac13 x + \\tfrac83,\\\\\n   L_5 &: y = -3x +11,\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   cover all fifteen points of \\(T_5\\) (a direct substitution check for each point of \\(T_5\\) confirms the claim).  \n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume that for some \\(m\\ge 3,\\;m\\neq4\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_m=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\}\\) of distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_m\\).  \n   We construct a family \\(\\mathcal L_{m+2}\\) of \\(m+2\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{m+2}\\).\n\n   1. **Keep the old lines.** The set \\(T_m\\subset T_{m+2}\\) is already covered by \\(\\mathcal L_m\\).  \n   2. **Add two new lines** that cover the newly appearing points, i.e. the points with either \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) or \\(a+b=m+3\\).  \n      Define  \n\n      \\[\n      \\begin{aligned}\n      L_{m+1}&:\\; \\text{the line through }(1,m+2)\\text{ and }(2,m+1),\\\\\n      L_{m+2}&:\\; \\text{the line through }(m+2,1)\\text{ and }(m+1,2).\n      \\end{aligned}\n      \\]\n\n      Their slopes are respectively \\(-\\tfrac12\\) and \\(-2\\); both are sunny.  \n      *Coverage check.*  \n      - The points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b=m+2\\) and \\((2,m+1)\\) lie on \\(L_{m+1}\\); similarly \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a=m+2\\) and \\((m+1,2)\\) lie on \\(L_{m+2}\\).  \n      - Any point \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le m+1\\) is already covered by a line of \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) (by the induction hypothesis).  \n      - Any point \\((a,1)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le m+1\\) is likewise already covered.  \n      - The points on the new diagonal \\(a+b=m+3\\) are exactly \\((1,m+2),(2,m+1),\\dots ,(m+2,1)\\); each of them belongs to either \\(L_{m+1}\\) or \\(L_{m+2}\\).  \n\n      Hence every point of \\(T_{m+2}\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{m}\\cup\\{L_{m+1},L_{m+2}\\}\\).  \n      The two new lines are distinct from all previous ones because they contain the previously unused column\u2011\\(1\\) point \\((1,m+2)\\) and row\u2011\\(1\\) point \\((m+2,1)\\).  \n\n   Consequently \\(T_{m+2}\\) can be covered by \\(m+2\\) sunny lines.  \n   Starting from the base case \\(m=3\\) and applying the step repeatedly we obtain coverings for all odd \\(m\\ge3\\) and, by one extra application of the step to the even base case \\(m=5\\), for all even \\(m\\ge6\\).  \n   Together with the explicit constructions for \\(m=0,1,3,5\\) we have proved:\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{For every }m\\ge0,\\;m\\neq2,4,\\;T_m\\text{ can be covered by exactly }m\\text{ sunny lines}.}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Putting everything together.**  \n   Let \\(n\\ge3\\) be given and let \\(k\\) be a non\u2011negative integer with \\(k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq2,4\\).\n\n   *Construction.*  \n   - Use \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  \n   - The points not yet covered are precisely the set \\(T_k\\).  \n   - By the result of step\u202f4, \\(T_k\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.  \n\n   The union of the two families consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n   *Impossibility of \\(k=2\\) and \\(k=4\\).*  \n   If a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines existed for \\(k=2\\) (resp. \\(k=4\\)), then after removing all non\u2011sunny lines we would obtain a covering of \\(T_k\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines, contradicting the impossibility proved in step\u202f3.\n\n   Therefore the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely all non\u2011negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\) except \\(2\\) and \\(4\\).\n\n**Final answer:**  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n,\\;k\\neq2,\\;k\\neq4\\,\\}} .\n\\]"

