elong to any line of \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) (the points \\((1,m+2)\\) and \\((m+2,1)\\) are not in \\(T_m\\)), therefore they are new and distinct from all lines already present.  Consequently \\(\\mathcal L_{m+2}\\) consists of exactly \\(m+2\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n   Thus, starting from any admissible base case we can increase the parameter by 2 while preserving the required properties.\n\n5. **Obtaining coverings for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Odd \\(k\\).*  The families for \\(k=1,3,5\\) serve as bases.  Repeated application of the inductive step of §4 produces coverings for all odd integers \\(k\\ge1\\) (the parity is preserved because we add two lines each time).\n\n   *Even \\(k\\).*  The families for \\(k=0,6\\) serve as bases.  Starting from the covering for \\(k=6\\) and applying the inductive step repeatedly yields coverings for every even integer \\(k\\ge6\\).  The case \\(k=0\\) is trivial.\n\n   Together with the impossibility results for \\(k=2,4\\) we have proved that a covering of \\(T_k\\) by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff** \\(k\\neq2,4\\).\n\n6. **Returning to the original problem.**  \n   For a given \\(n\\ge3\\) and a non‑negative integer  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{2,4\\},\n   \\]\n\n   take the \\(k\\) sunny lines that cover \\(T_k\\) (constructed in §§3–5) and add the vertical non‑sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   The vertical lines cover all points with first coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\); the sunny lines cover the remaining points, which are precisely the translated set \\(T_k\\).  Hence every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   The impossibility proofs for \\(k=2,4\\) show that no configuration with exactly two or four sunny lines can exist for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Reduction to the set \\(T_k\\)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) and let \\(k\\) be a non‑negative integer with \\(k\\le n\\).\nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k\\,\\},\n\\]\n\nconsisting of \\(n-k\\) distinct vertical lines (vertical lines are not sunny).\nEvery lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal V\\).\n\nHence the only points that are not yet covered are those with \\(a>n-k\\).\nWrite  \n\n\\[\na'=a-(n-k),\\qquad b'=b .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\na'+b' = (a-(n-k))+b \\le (n+1-(n-k)) = k+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered points are exactly  \n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(a',b')\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a'+b'\\le k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently:\n\n> **Problem \\(*\\).**  There exist \\(n\\) lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines satisfying the original condition **iff** there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_k\\).\n\nFrom now on we work only with \\(T_k\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Impossibility for \\(k=2\\) and \\(k=4\\)\n\n*Case \\(k=2\\).*  \n\\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nThe three possible lines through two of these points have slopes  \n\n\\[\n\\infty\\;(x=1),\\;0\\;(y=1),\\;-1\\;(y=-x+3),\n\\]\n\nall of which are forbidden for a sunny line.  Hence a sunny line can contain at most one of the three points, so at least three sunny lines are needed.  Therefore a covering with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist.\n\n*Case \\(k=4\\).*  \n\\(T_4\\) consists of the ten points  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),\\\\\n&(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),\\\\\n&(3,1),(3,2),\\\\\n&(4,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is never vertical, so it can contain at most one point of the first column \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid1\\le b\\le4\\}\\); consequently at least four sunny lines are required, and each of them must also contain a distinct point of the first row \\(\\{(a,1)\\mid1\\le a\\le4\\}\\).\n\nThus the four lines must be determined by the four unordered pairs  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,b_i),(a_i,1)\\},\\qquad i=1,2,3,4,\n\\]\n\nwith \\(\\{a_i\\}=\\{b_i\\}=\\{2,3,4\\}\\) and \\(a_i\\neq b_i\\) (otherwise the line would be horizontal or have slope \\(-1\\)).  The only possible pairings are the two derangements of \\(\\{2,3,4\\}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{pairing}&\\text{slopes}\\\\ \\hline\n(2,3),(3,4),(4,2) & -2,\\ -\\tfrac32,\\ -\\tfrac13\\\\\n(2,4),(3,2),(4,3) & -3,\\ -\\tfrac12,\\ -\\tfrac23\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nIn both cases the interior points \\((2,3)\\) and \\((3,2)\\) are not on any of the three lines listed above; the fourth line must pass through \\((1,1)\\) and, to stay sunny, can only be \\(y=x\\), which adds the point \\((2,2)\\) but still leaves \\((2,3)\\) and \\((3,2)\\) uncovered.  Hence no family of four sunny lines can cover \\(T_4\\).\n\nTherefore a covering with exactly two or four sunny lines is impossible for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Explicit families for the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\nBelow we list, for each admissible \\(k\\), a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that covers \\(T_k\\).  \nAll slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently every line is sunny.\n\n*Notation.*  For a point \\((x_0,y_0)\\) and a slope \\(m\\) we write the line as \\(y-y_0=m(x-x_0)\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 3.1  The families\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|l}\nk & \\mathcal L_k \\\\ \\hline\n0 & \\varnothing \\\\[2mm]\n1 & \\{\\,L_1:\\;y=x\\,\\} \\\\[2mm]\n3 & \\{\\,L_1:\\;y=x,\\;\n        L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\;\n        L_3:\\;y=-2x+5\\,\\} \\\\[2mm]\n5 & \\{\\,L_1:\\;y=x,\\;\n        L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac72,\\;\n        L_3:\\;y=-2x+7,\\;\n        L_4:\\;y=-\\tfrac13x+\\tfrac83,\\;\n        L_5:\\;y=-3x+11\\,\\} \\\\[2mm]\n6 & \\{\\,L_1:\\;y=x,\\;\n        L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac72,\\;\n        L_3:\\;y=-2x+7,\\;\n        L_4:\\;y=-\\tfrac13x+\\tfrac{10}{3},\\;\n        L_5:\\;y=-3x+11,\\;\n        L_6:\\;y=-\\tfrac14x+\