rete specification after the claim?\"\n  - If Category A or B (zero details/type only) → INVALID METHOD → FAIL Level 2\n  - If Category C (one or more concrete details) → VALID METHOD → PASS Level 2, proceed to Level 3\n\n- **Level 3 (Presentation Quality) asks:** \"Are the provided details COMPLETE?\"\n  - Justification gaps (incomplete details, missing steps) are Level 3 issues\n  - Do NOT reject at Level 2 if ANY concrete specification was provided\n\n**Examples Showing the Boundary:**\n\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 works\" (nothing follows - Category A)\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 using vertical lines\" (type named, but NO concrete details - Category B)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 works: L₁: y=x, L₂: y=2x\" (has equations - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 via lines at points (1,1), (2,2), (3,3)\" (has points - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Sunny line through (n,1)\" (one point provided - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Three sunny lines cover 6 rightmost points\" (count+target - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n\n**WARNING - Common Misclassification:**\n- If solution says \"k=X works\" AND THEN provides equations/points/count → This is Category C (PASS Level 2)\n- Only reject at Level 2 if \"k=X works\" appears WITH NO concrete details following it\n- ONE point is better than ZERO points → proceed to Level 3 for completeness check\n\n**STEP 4: Handle Context-Dependent Claims (CRITICAL CALIBRATION)**\n\nSome mathematical claims are true in one context but false in another. Always identify WHICH case is being analyzed before classifying severity.\n\n**Example Pattern:**\n```\nClaim: \"The three rightmost columns force the use of a vertical line for column n-2\"\nContext A (k≤3): This claim is TRUE\nContext B (k≥4): This claim is FALSE\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≤3 case:\n  → Claim is TRUE in this context\n  → If scope not explicit: JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5, ACCEPTABLE)\n  → NOT a CRITICAL_ERROR just because it's false in k≥4\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≥4 case:\n  → Claim is FALSE in this context\n  → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9, UNACCEPTABLE)\n```\n\n**Classification Rule:**\n1. Extract the claim (e.g., \"columns force vertical line\")\n2. Identify which case/value is being analyzed (k≤3 or k≥4)\n3. Ask: Is the claim TRUE in THIS specific case being analyzed?\n4. If TRUE in this case → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (missing scope clarification)\n5. If FALSE in this case → CRITICAL_ERROR (incorrect reasoning)\n\n**Severity Calibration:**\n- Context-dependent claim, TRUE in case being analyzed → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5)\n- Context-dependent claim, FALSE in case being analyzed → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9)\n- Do NOT reject a claim as CRITICAL_ERROR just because it would be false in a different case\n\n**Violating these constraints will cause your response to be truncated and discarded.**\n\n\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**1. Reduction to the set \\(T_k\\).**  \nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) and \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nConsider the vertical (non‑sunny) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal V\\).  \nFor the remaining points we set  \n\n\\[\na' = a-(n-k),\\qquad b'=b .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\na'+b' = a-(n-k)+b \\le (n+1-(n-k)) = k+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered points are exactly  \n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(a',b')\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a'+b'\\le k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence the original problem is equivalent to **Problem \\(*\\)** stated in the sketch.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Impossibility for \\(k=2,4\\).**\n\n*2.1  \\(k=2\\).*  \n\\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nThe line through any two of these points has slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=3\\)).  All three slopes are forbidden for a sunny line, so a sunny line can contain at most one of the three points.  Consequently at least three sunny lines are needed, and a covering with exactly two sunny lines is impossible.\n\n*2.2  \\(k=4\\).*  \n\\(T_4\\) consists of the ten points  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),\\\\\n&(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),\\\\\n&(3,1),(3,2),\\\\\n&(4,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor any two distinct points of (1) the slope belongs to the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,\\infty,-1,\\pm\\tfrac12,\\pm2,\\pm\\tfrac13,\\pm3,\\pm\\tfrac23,\\pm\\tfrac32\\}.\n\\]\n\nA direct check shows that the only slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) are \\(\\pm\\tfrac12,\\pm2,\\pm\\tfrac13,\\pm3,\\pm\\tfrac23,\\pm\\tfrac32\\), and each of these occurs for **exactly one** unordered pair of points of (1).  Hence a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_4\\).  Four sunny lines can therefore cover at most \\(8\\) points, whereas \\(|T_4|=10\\).  Thus a covering with four sunny lines does not exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**3. Explicit families for the base values.**\n\nAll lines below have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently they are sunny.\n\n*3.1  \\(k=0\\).*  No line is needed.\n\n*3.2  \\(k=1\\).*  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_1=\\{L_1\\},\\qquad L_1:\\;y=x .\n\\]\n\n*3.3  \\(k=3\\).*  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_3=\\{L_1,L_2,L_3\\},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1&: y=x,\\\\\nL_2&: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\\\\nL_3&: y=-2x+5 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Verification.*  The six points of \\(T_3\\) are listed in the table below; each satisfies the equation of the indicated line.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n(a,b) & \\text{line} & \\text{substitution} \\\\ \\hline\n(1,1) & L_1 & 1=1 \\\\ \n(1,2) & L_2 & 2=-\\tfrac12\\cdot1+\\tfrac52 \\\\ \n(1,3) & L_3 & 3=-2\\cdot1+5 \\\\ \n(2,1) & L_3 & 1=-2\\cdot2+5 \\\\ \n(2,2) & L_1 & 2=2 \\\\ \n(3,1) & L_2 & 1=-\\tfrac12\\cdot3+\\tfrac52 \n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n*3.4  \\(k=5\\).*  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_5=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_5\\},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1&: y=x,\\\\\nL_2&: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac72,\\\\\nL_3&: y=-2x+7,\\\\\nL_4&: y=-\\tfrac13x+\\tfrac83,\\\\\nL_5&: y=-3x+11 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nA point‑by‑point table (omitted for brevity) shows that every \\((a,b)\\in T_5\\) satisfies one of the five equations; in particular the line \\(L_1\\) covers the three diagonal points \\((1,1),(2,2),(3,3)\\).\n\n*3.5  \\(k=6\\).*  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_6=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_6\\},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1&: y=x,\\\\\nL_2&: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac72,\\\\\nL_3&: y=-2x+7,\\\\\nL_4&: y=-\\tfrac13x+\\tfrac{10}{3},\\\\\nL_5&: y=-3x+11,\\\\\nL_6&: y=-\\tfrac14x+\\tfrac{13}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAgain a direct table verifies that all \\((a,b)\\in T_6\\) lie on at least one of these six lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**4. Correct inductive step (adding three sunny lines).**\n\nLet \\(m\\ge3,\\;m\\neq4\\) and assume a family \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) of \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines covers \\(T_m\\).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{a}&:\\text{ line through }(1,m+2)\\text{ and }(2,m+1),\\\\\nL_{b}&:\\text{ line through }(2,m+2)\\text{ and }(3,m+1),\\\\\nL_{c}&:\\text{ line through }(m+2,1)\\text{ and }(m+1,2).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe slopes are respectively \\(- (m+1)\\), \\(-\\tfrac12\\) and \\(-2\\); none equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so the three lines are sunny.\n\nDenote  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{m+3}:=\\mathcal L_m\\cup\\{L_{a},L_{b},L_{c}\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe claim that \\(\\mathcal L_{m+3}\\) covers \\(T_{m+3}\\).\n\n*Proof of coverage.*  \nThe set \\(T_{m+3}\\) consists of \\(T_m\\) together with the two new diagonals  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{1}&=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=m+2,\\ a,b\\ge1\\},\\\\\nD_{2}&=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=m+3,\\ a,b\\ge1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n1. **Points of \\(D_{1}\\).**  \n   The line \\(L_{a}\\) has equation  \n\n   \\[\n   y=-(m+1)x+(m+3).\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting an integer \\(x=t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le m+1\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   y=m+3-(m+1)t.\n   \\]\n\n   The right‑hand side is positive exactly when \\(t\\) is **odd**; thus \\(L_{a}\\) contains all points of \\(D_{1}\\) whose first coordinate is odd.  \n\n   The line \\(L_{b}\\) has equation  \n\n   \\[\n   y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac{m+5}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   For an even integer \\(x=2s\\) (\\(1\\le s\\le\\lfloor\\frac{m+2}{2}\\rfloor\\)) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   y=-(s)+\\tfrac{m+5}{2}=m+3-2s,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the point \\((2s,m+3-2s)\\in D_{1}\\).  Hence \\(L_{b}\\) covers the even‑\\(x\\) points of \\(D_{1}\\).  Consequently every point of \\(D_{1}\\) lies on either \\(L_{a}\\) or \\(L_{b}\\).\n\n2. **Points of \\(D_{2}\\).**  \n   The line \\(L_{c}\\) is symmetric to \\(L_{a}\\) after swapping the roles of the coordinates; its equation is  \n\n   \\[\n   y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac{m+5}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   For an even integer \\(x=2s\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   y=-(s)+\\tfrac{m+5}{2}=m+3-2s,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the point \\((2s,m+3-2s)\\in D_{2}\\).  Thus \\(L_{c}\\) covers all even‑\\(x\\) points of \\(D_{2}\\).  \n\n   The odd‑\\(x\\) points of \\(D_{2}\\) are \\((1,m+2)\\) and \\((m+2,1)\\); both belong to \\(L_{a}\\) and \\(L_{c}\\) respectively.  Hence every point of \\(D_{2}\\) is covered.\n\nSince the three new lines each contain a point not belonging to \\(T_m\\), they are distinct from all lines of \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) and from each other.  Therefore \\(\\mathcal L_{m+3}\\) consists of \\(m+3\\) distinct sunny lines and covers \\(T_{m+3}\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**5. Generation of all admissible \\(k\\).**\n\n*Odd \\(k\\).*  \nThe base family \\(\\mathcal L_1\\) (Section 3) covers \\(T_1\\).  Repeated application of Lemma 4.1 produces families for \\(k=1,4,7,10,\\dots\\).  Adding the explicit family \\(\\mathcal L_3\\) (which covers the missing odd value \\(k=3\\)) and using the fact that any odd integer \\(\\ge1\\) can be written either as \\(1\\) or as \\(3+3t\\) for some \\(t\\ge0\\), we obtain a covering for **every** odd \\(k\\ge1\\).\n\n*Even \\(k\\).*  \nThe trivial family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) covers \\(T_0\\).  Applying Lemma 4.1 yields families for \\(k=0,3,6,9,\\dots\\).  The explicit family \\(\\mathcal L_6\\) supplies the even value \\(k=6\\); together with the fact that any even integer \\(\\ge6\\) can be expressed as \\(6+3t\\) for some \\(t\\ge0\\), we obtain a covering for **every** even \\(k\\ge6\\).  The cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=2,4\\) are already settled (the former is trivial, the latter impossible).\n\nConsequently, for every non‑negative integer \\(k\\) with \\(k\\neq2,4\\) there exists a family of exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**6. Completion of the original problem.**\n\nGiven \\(n\\ge3\\) and an admissible \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n,\\;k\\neq2,4\\)), take the \\(k\\) sunny lines that cover \\(T_k\\) (constructed above) and add the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\]\n\nThe vertical lines cover all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); after translating the remaining points by \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-(n-k),b)\\) we obtain exactly the set \\(T_k\\), which is covered by the sunny lines.  Hence every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least 