f\\(*\\)** stated above.\n\n2. **Impossibility for \\(k=2,4\\).**\n\n   *\\(k=2\\).*  \\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n   The line through any two of these points has slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=3\\)).  All three slopes are forbidden for a sunny line, so a sunny line can contain at most one of the three points.  Hence at least three sunny lines are needed; a covering with exactly two sunny lines is impossible.\n\n   *\\(k=4\\).*  \\(T_4\\) consists of the ten points  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),\\\\\n   &(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),\\\\\n   &(3,1),(3,2),\\\\\n   &(4,1).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   For any unordered pair of distinct points of (1) the slope belongs to  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{0,\\infty,-1,\\pm\\tfrac12,\\pm2,\\pm\\tfrac13,\\pm3,\\pm\\tfrac23,\\pm\\tfrac32\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The only slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) are the six values \\(\\pm\\tfrac12,\\pm2,\\pm\\tfrac13,\\pm3,\\pm\\tfrac23,\\pm\\tfrac32\\), and each of them occurs for **exactly one** pair of points of (1).  Hence a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_4\\).  Four sunny lines can therefore cover at most \\(8\\) points, whereas \\(|T_4|=10\\).  Thus a covering with four sunny lines does not exist.\n\n3. **Explicit families for the base values.**  \n   All lines below have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently they are sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  No line is needed.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_1=\\{L_1\\},\\qquad L_1:\\;y=x .\n   \\]\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_3=\\{L_1,L_2,L_3\\},\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1&: y=x,\\\\\n   L_2&: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\\\\n   L_3&: y=-2x+5 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   The six points of \\(T_3\\) are listed in the table below; each satisfies the equation of the indicated line.\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n   (a,b) & \\text{line} & \\text{substitution} \\\\ \\hline\n   (1,1) & L_1 & 1=1 \\\\ \n   (1,2) & L_2 & 2=-\\tfrac12\\cdot1+\\tfrac52 \\\\ \n   (1,3) & L_3 & 3=-2\\cdot1+5 \\\\ \n   (2,1) & L_3 & 1=-2\\cdot2+5 \\\\ \n   (2,2) & L_1 & 2=2 \\\\ \n   (3,1) & L_2 & 1=-\\tfrac12\\cdot3+\\tfrac52 \n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   *\\(k=5\\).*  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_5=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_5\\},\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1&: y=x,\\\\\n   L_2&: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac72,\\\\\n   L_3&: y=-2x+7,\\\\\n   L_4&: y=-\\tfrac13x+\\tfrac83,\\\\\n   L_5&: y=-3x+11 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   A direct verification (omitted for brevity) shows that every point of \\(T_5\\) lies on at least one of these five lines.\n\n   *\\(k=6\\).*  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_6=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_6\\},\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1&: y=x,\\\\\n   L_2&: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac72,\\\\\n   L_3&: y=-2x+7,\\\\\n   L_4&: y=-\\tfrac13x+\\tfrac{10}{3},\\\\\n   L_5&: y=-3x+11,\\\\\n   L_6&: y=-\\tfrac14x+\\tfrac{13}{2}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Again a point\u2011by\u2011point check confirms that all points of \\(T_6\\) are covered.\n\n4. **Lemma 4.1 (adding three sunny lines).**  \n\n   *Statement.*  Let \\(m\\ge3\\) and \\(m\\neq4\\). Suppose a family \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) of \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines covers \\(T_m\\). Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_{1}&:\\text{ through }(1,m+2)\\text{ and }(2,m+1),\\\\\n   L_{2}&:\\text{ through }(2,m+2)\\text{ and }(3,m+1),\\\\\n   L_{3}&:\\text{ through }(m+2,1)\\text{ and }(m+1,2).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Their slopes are \\(-(m+1),\\;-\\tfrac12,\\; -2\\), none of which equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus the three lines are sunny.  \n   Then  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{m+3}:=\\mathcal L_m\\cup\\{L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   is a family of \\(m+3\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_{m+3}\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  The set \\(T_{m+3}\\) consists of \\(T_m\\) together with the two new diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{1}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=m+2,\\ a,b\\ge1\\},\\qquad\n   D_{2}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=m+3,\\ a,b\\ge1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   1. **Coverage of \\(D_{1}\\).**  \n      The line \\(L_{1}\\) has equation \\(y=-(m+1)x+(m+3)\\).  \n      Substituting an integer \\(x=t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le m+1\\) gives \\(y=m+3-(m+1)t\\), which is positive exactly when \\(t\\) is odd.  Hence \\(L_{1}\\) contains all points of \\(D_{1}\\) whose first coordinate is odd.  \n\n      The line \\(L_{2}\\) has equation \\(y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac{m+5}{2}\\).  \n      For an even integer \\(x=2s\\) (\\(1\\le s\\le\\lfloor\\frac{m+2}{2}\\rfloor\\)) we obtain \\(y=m+3-2s\\), i.e. the point \\((2s,m+3-2s)\\in D_{1}\\).  Thus \\(L_{2}\\) contains the even\u2011first\u2011coordinate points of \\(D_{1}\\).  \n\n      Consequently every point of \\(D_{1}\\) lies on either \\(L_{1}\\) or \\(L_{2}\\).\n\n   2. **Coverage of \\(D_{2}\\).**  \n      The line \\(L_{3}\\) is symmetric to \\(L_{1}\\) after swapping the axes; its equation is \\(y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac{m+5}{2}\\).  \n      For an even integer \\(x=2s\\) we obtain \\(y=m+3-2s\\), i.e. the point \\((2s,m+3-2s)\\in D_{2}\\).  Hence \\(L_{3}\\) covers all even\u2011first\u2011coordinate points of \\(D_{2}\\).  \n\n      The two odd\u2011first\u2011coordinate points of \\(D_{2}\\) are \\((1,m+2)\\) and \\((m+2,1)\\); they belong to \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{3}\\) respectively.  Thus every point of \\(D_{2}\\) is covered.\n\n   3. **Distinctness.**  \n      Each of the three new lines contains a point (e.g. \\((1,m+2)\\) for \\(L_{1}\\)) that does not belong to \\(T_m\\); therefore none of them coincides with a line of \\(\\mathcal L_m\\).  They are also pairwise distinct because their slopes are different.  \n\n   Since the old points remain covered by \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) and the new points are covered by the three added lines, \\(\\mathcal L_{m+3}\\) covers \\(T_{m+3}\\) and consists of \\(m+3\\) distinct sunny lines. \u220e\n\n5. **Generation of all admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Let \\(k\\ge0\\) with \\(k\\neq2,4\\). Write \\(k\\) according to its residue modulo \\(3\\):\n\n   * If \\(k\\equiv0\\pmod3\\) then \\(k=0+3t\\) with \\(t=k/3\\).  \n   * If \\(k\\equiv1\\pmod3\\) then \\(k=1+3t\\) with \\(t=(k-1)/3\\).  \n   * If \\(k\\equiv2\\pmod3\\) then \\(k\\ge5\\) (the only excluded values are \\(2\\) and \\(4\\)); write \\(k=5+3t\\) with \\(t=(k-5)/3\\).\n\n   Thus every admissible \\(k\\) can be expressed as \\(s+3t\\) where \\(s\\in\\{0,1,3,5,6\\}\\) and \\(t\\ge0\\).\n\n   For each \\(s\\) we have a concrete family \\(\\mathcal L_s\\) covering \\(T_s\\) (step\u202f3).  \n   Applying Lemma\u202f4.1 repeatedly \\(t\\) times to \\(\\mathcal L_s\\) yields a family \\(\\mathcal L_{s+3t}\\) of exactly \\(s+3t=k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\).  Hence a covering exists for every admissible \\(k\\).\n\n6. **Completion of the original problem.**  \n\n   For the given \\(n\\ge3\\) and admissible \\(k\\) (i.e. \\(0\\le k\\le n,\\;k\\neq2,4\\)) we take the \\(k\\) sunny lines that cover \\(T_k\\) (constructed above) and add the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   The vertical lines cover all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); after the translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-(n-k),b)\\) the remaining points are exactly \\(T_k\\), which are covered by the sunny lines.  Thus every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and precisely \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   The impossibility proofs for \\(k=2,4\\) show that no configuration with exactly two or four sunny lines can exist for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n   Consequently the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n,\\;k\\neq2,\\;k\\neq4\\,\\}} .\n   \\] \n\n\u220e"
