ough (2,3) and (4,2). (4,2) is in T_5. So we could use that line to cover (2,3) and (4,2). However, (2,3) is already covered by slope -2 line (1,5),(2,3),(3,1). So maybe we can adjust.\n\n- Slope -1 line is forbidden.\n\n- Slope -1/2 line also covers (1,4) and (3,3)? Let's check: (1,4) and (3,3) slope (3-4)/(3-1) = -0.5, line y = -0.5x + 4.5? Actually compute: y = -0.5x + b, plug (1,4): 4 = -0.5 + b => b = 4.5. So line y = -0.5x + 4.5 passes through (1,4) and (3,3). (3,3) is covered by y=x. So we could use that line to cover (1,4) and (3,3). But (3,3) is already covered.\n\n- Slope -2 line also covers (4,? ) maybe (4,-? ) not needed.\n\n- Slope 0.5 line also covers (1,0.5) not integer.\n\n- Slope -3 line also covers (3,? ) not needed.\n\nThus we can try to cover all points with 5 lines:\n\nLet's propose the following set:\n\n1. L1: y = x (covers (1,1),(2,2),(3,3))\n2. L2: y = -0.5x + 3.5 (covers (1,3),(3,2),(5,1))\n3. L3: y = -2x + 7 (covers (1,5),(2,3),(3,1))\n4. L4: y = 2x (covers (1,2),(2,4))\n5. L5: y = -0.5x + 2.5 (covers (1,2)? Actually (1,2) already covered by L4. But we need to cover (1,4),(2,1),(4,1),(4,2). Let's see if we can cover these with L5: maybe y = -0.5x + 4.5 (covers (1,4),(3,3)). But (3,3) already covered. So L5 could be y = -0.5x + 4.5 covering (1,4) and (3,3). That covers (1,4). Good. Now we still need to cover (2,1),(4,1),(4,2). (4,2) is covered by L2? Actually L2 covers (5,1) and (3,2) and (1,3). So (4,2) not covered. L3 covers (1,5),(2,3),(3,1). So (4,2) not covered. L4 covers (1,2),(2,4). So (4,2) not covered. L5 covers (1,4),(3,3). So (4,2) still uncovered. (2,1) also uncovered. (4,1) uncovered.\n\nWe need to adjust lines to cover these. Perhaps we can replace L4 with a line that covers (2,1) and (4,2) as we found: y = 0.5x. That covers (2,1) and (4,2). So L4: y = 0.5x. Then (1,2) and (2,4) remain uncovered. But maybe we can cover (1,2) and (2,4) with L5: y = 2x (covers (1,2),(2,4)). So we can set L4 = y = 0.5x, L5 = y = 2x. Then we have lines:\n\n- L1: y = x (covers (1,1),(2,2),(3,3))\n- L2: y = -0.5x + 3.5 (covers (1,3),(3,2),(5,1))\n- L3: y = -2x + 7 (covers (1,5),(2,3),(3,1))\n- L4: y = 0.5x (covers (2,1),(4,2))\n- L5: y = 2x (covers (1,2),(2,4))\n\nNow we still need to cover (1,4) and (4,1). (1,4) is not covered. (4,1) is not covered. Could we adjust L2 or L3 to also cover (1,4) or (4,1)? Let's see:\n\n- L2: y = -0.5x + 3.5 passes through (1,3) and (3,2) and (5,1). It does not pass through (1,4) (y=4 vs 3.5). So not.\n\n- L3: y = -2x + 7 passes through (1,5),(2,3),(3,1). Not (1,4) or (4,1). For x=4, y = -8+7 = -1, not.\n\n- L4: y = 0.5x passes through (4,2) but not (4,1) or (1,4). For x=1, y=0.5, not.\n\n- L5: y = 2x passes through (1,2) and (2,4). For x=4, y=8, not.\n\n- L1: y = x passes through (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). Not (1,4) or (4,1).\n\nThus we still have two points uncovered. We need to cover them with one line (since we have used 5 lines). Perhaps we can replace one of the lines with a line that covers both (1,4) and (4,1). The line through (1,4) and (4,1) has slope (1-4)/(4-1) = -3/3 = -1, which is forbidden (parallel to x+y=0). So cannot pair them. So we need separate lines for each, but we have no extra line. So maybe we need a line that covers (1,4) together with some other point already covered, allowing us to drop a line. For example, line through (1,4) and (3,2) slope (2-4)/(3-1) = -1, forbidden. (1,4) and (2,3) slope (3-4)/(2-1) = -1, forbidden. (1,4) and (2,2) slope (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. So line through (1,4) and (2,2) is y = -2x + 6. That line also passes through (3,0) not needed. So we could use line y = -2x + 6 to cover (1,4) and (2,2). (2,2) is already covered by L1 (y=x). So we could replace L1 with L1' = y = -2x + 6? But then we lose coverage of (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). However, (1,1) could be covered by some other line? Let's see.\n\nAlternatively, we could replace L1 (y=x) with a line that covers (1,1) and (2,2) and maybe (4,1) or (1,4). But (1,1) and (2,2) are on y=x. (1,1) and (4,1) slope (1-1)/(4-1) = 0, forbidden. (1,1) and (1,4) vertical, forbidden. So cannot combine.\n\nMaybe we can replace L3 (y = -2x + 7) with a line that also covers (4,1). Let's see line through (4,1) and (2,3) slope (3-1)/(2-4) = 2/(-2) = -1, forbidden. (4,1) and (3,1) slope 0, forbidden. (4,1) and (1,5) slope (5-1)/(1-4) = 4/(-3) = -4/3, allowed. So line through (1,5) and (4,1) has slope -4/3. Equation: y = -4/3 x + b, plug (1,5): 5 = -4/3 + b => b = 5 + 4/3 = 19/3 ≈ 6.333. So line y = -4/3 x + 19/3 passes through (1,5) and (4,1). Does it also pass through any other points? For x=2, y = -8/3 + 19/3 = 11/3 ≈ 3.667, not integer. x=3 => y = -12/3 + 19/3 = 7/3 ≈ 2.333. So not integer. So this line covers (1,5) and (4,1). (1,5) is already covered by L3. So we could replace L3 with this line to also cover (4,1). Then we still need to cover (1,4). Could we cover (1,4) with L2? L2 is y = -0.5x + 3.5, which at x=1 gives y=3, not 4. So not. Could we cover (1,4) with L5? L5 is y = 2x, at x=1 gives y=2. Not. L4 is y = 0.5x, at x=1 gives y=0.5. Not. L1 is y=x, at x=1 gives y=1. Not. So we still need to cover (1,4). Could we replace L2 with a line that covers (1,4) and also some other points? For example, line through (1,4) and (3,2) is slope -1, forbidden. (1,4) and (2,3) slope -1, forbidden. (1,4) and (3,1) slope (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2 = -1.5, allowed. So line through (1,4) and (3,1) has slope -1.5 = -3/2. Equation: y = -1.5x + b, plug (1,4): 4 = -1.5 + b => b = 5.5 = 11/2. So line y = -3/2 x + 11/2 passes through (1,4) and (3,1). (3,1) is already covered by L3 originally, but we replaced L3 with line covering (1,5) and (4,1). So (3,1) is now uncovered. But we could cover (3,1) with this new line L2' (the -3/2 line). So we could replace L2 with L2' = y = -3/2 x + 11/2, covering (1,4) and (3,1). Then we need to cover (1,3) and (3,2) and (5,1) which were previously covered by L2. We need to cover them elsewhere. Perhaps L3 (the -4/3 line) does not cover them. L1 (y=x) does not. L4 (0.5x) does not. L5 (2x) does not. So we need to cover (1,3),(3,2),(5,1) with some other line. Could we use L5 (2x) to cover (1,3)? No. Could we use L4 (0.5x) to cover (5,1)? No. So we need to adjust further.\n\nThus constructing a covering with 5 lines is nontrivial. But maybe it's possible. Let's try a systematic approach: we need to cover 15 points with 5 lines, average 3 points per line. Some lines can cover 3 points (like slope -0.5 line covering (1,3),(3,2),(5,1) and slope -2 line covering (1,5),(2,3),(3,1)). That's 6 points covered by 2 lines. Then we have 9 points left: (1,1),(1,2),(1,4),(2,1),(2,2),(2,4),(3,3),(4,1),(4,2). We need to cover these with 3 lines. Let's see if we can find 3 lines covering these 9 points.\n\nPotential lines:\n\n- y = x covers (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). That's 3 points.\n\n- y = 2x covers (1,2),(2,4). That's 2 points.\n\n- y = 0.5x covers (2,1),(4,2). That's 2 points.\n\n- y = -2x + 6 covers (1,4),(2,2) (but (2,2) already covered). So we could use that line to cover (1,4) and maybe (2,2) but (2,2) already covered. So we could replace y=x with something else? Let's see.\n\nWe have left after using y=x, 2x, 0.5x: points covered: (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) by y=x; (1,2),(2,4) by 2x; (2,1),(4,2) by 0.5x. Uncovered: (1,4),(4,1). That's two points left, but we have used 5 lines already (including the two 3-point lines). Actually we have used 5 lines: L1 (slope -0.5 line covering (1,3),(3,2),(5,1)), L2 (slope -2 line covering (1,5),(2,3),(3,1)), L3 (y=x), L4 (y=2x), L5 (y=0.5x). That's 5 lines. Uncovered points: (1,4) and (4,1). So we need to adjust to cover them within the 5 lines.\n\nWe could try to replace one of the lines with a line that covers one of these points plus some other points, while still covering all points. For instance, replace y=2x (covers (1,2),(2,4)) with a line that covers (1,2),(2,4) and also (1,4) maybe? Let's see if there is a line through (1,2) and (1,4) vertical, forbidden. So cannot.\n\nReplace y=0.5x (covers (2,1),(4,2)) with a line that covers (2,1),(4,2) and (4,1) maybe? Let's see line through (2,1) and (4,1) slope (1-1)/(4-2)=0, forbidden. So no.\n\nReplace y=x (covers (1,1),(2,2),(3,3)) with a line that covers (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) and also (1,4) or (4,1). Not possible because (1,4) not collinear with those points. So we need a different approach.\n\nMaybe we can use a line that covers (1,4) and (3,2) (but slope -1, forbidden). Or (1,4) and (2,2) slope -2, allowed. So line y = -2x + 6 covers (1,4) and (2,2). (2,2) is currently covered by y=x. So we could replace y=x with line y = -2x + 6, but then we lose coverage of (1,1) and (3,3). However, (1,1) could be covered by some other line? Maybe we can cover (1,1) with line through (1,1) and (5,1) slope 0, forbidden. (1,1) and (3,1) slope 0, forbidden. (1,1) and (2,3) slope (3-1)/(2-1)=2, allowed. So line y = 2x -1 passes through (1,1) and (2,3). (2,3) is already covered by slope -2 line (1,5),(2,3),(3,1). So we could replace y=x with line y = 2x -1 covering (1,1) and (2,3). But (2,3) is already covered, but we need to also cover (3,3) maybe? Let's see line y = 2x -1 at x=3 gives y=5, not (3,3). So (3,3) would be uncovered. Could we cover (3,3) with some other line? Maybe line through (3,3) and (5,1) slope (1-3)/(5-3) = -2/2 = -1, forbidden. (3,3) and (4,1) slope (1-3)/(4-3) = -2, allowed. So line y = -2x + 9 passes through (3,3) and (4,1). Check: x=3 => y= -6+9=3, ok. x=4 => y= -8+9=1, ok. So line y = -2x + 9 covers (3,3) and (4,1). So we could use that line to cover (4,1) and (3,3). Then we could replace y=x with something else.\n\nThus we can try to construct a covering with 5 lines as follows:\n\n- L1: slope -0.5 line covering (1,3),(3,2),(5,1). (Equation: y = -0.5x + 3.5)\n- L2: slope -2 line covering (1,5),(2,3),(3,1). (Equation: y = -2x + 7)\n- L3: line y = 2x - 1 covering (1,1) and (2,3) (but (2,3) already covered by L2). So L3 covers (1,1) only new.\n- L4: line y = -2x + 6 covering (1,4) and (2,2). (Equation: y = -2x + 6)\n- L5: line y = -2x + 9 covering (3,3) and (4,1). (Equation: y = -2x + 9)\n\nNow we need to cover remaining points: (1,2),(2,1),(2,4),(4,2). Also (2,2) is covered by L4, (3,2) covered by L1, (5,1) covered by L1, (1,5) covered by L2, (2,3) covered by L2, (3,1) covered by L2, (1,3) covered by L1, (3,2) covered by L1, (5,1) covered by L1, (1,4) covered by L4, (2,2) covered by L4, (3,3) covered by L5, (4,1) covered by L5. So uncovered: (1,2),(2,1),(2,4),(4,2). That's 4 points left, but we have used all 5 lines. So we need to incorporate these points into existing lines.\n\nWe can try to adjust L3 (2x - 1) to also cover (2,1) maybe? Let's see line through (2,1) and (1,1) slope (1-1)/(2-1)=0, forbidden. So cannot.\n\nWe can adjust L4 (y = -2x + 6) to also cover (2,4) maybe? For x=2, y = -4+6=2, not 4. So no.\n\nWe can adjust L5 (y = -2x + 9) to also cover (2,4)? For x=2, y = -4+9=5, not 4.\n\nWe can adjust L1 (y = -0.5x + 3.5) to also cover (2,4)? For x=2, y = -1 + 3.5 = 2.5, not 4.\n\nThus this set fails.\n\nThus constructing a covering with 5 lines is challenging. But maybe it's impossible? Let's try to find a lower bound: For T_5, we need at least ceil(|T_5| / max points per line). The maximum number of points a sunny line can contain in T_5 might be 3 (as we saw). Could any line contain 4 points? Let's check possibilities. For a line to contain 4 points, the points must be collinear with slope not 0, ∞, -1. Let's search for any 4 collinear points in T_5. The points are arranged in a triangular shape. The line y = -0.5x + 3.5 contains (1,3),(3,2),(5,1) - 3 points. Could we add (2,? ) to this line? For x=2, y = -1 + 3.5 = 2.5, not integer. So no. The line y = -2x + 7 contains (1,5),(2,3),(3,1) - 3 points. Could we add (4,-1) not in T_5. So max 3. The line y = x contains (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) - 3 points. Could we add (4,4) not in T_5. So max 3. The line y = 2x contains (1,2),(2,4) - 2 points. The line y = 0.5x contains (2,1),(4,2) - 2 points. The line y = -2x + 6 contains (1,4),(2,2) - 2 points. The line y = -2x + 9 contains (3,3),(4,1) - 2 points. The line y = -3x + 11 contains (1,5),(2,2) - 2 points. The line y = -3/2 x + 11/2 contains (1,4),(3,1) - 2 points. The line y = -3/2 x + 13/2 contains (1,5),(3,2) - 2 points. The line y = -1/3 x + something maybe covers (1,4),(4,? ) not integer. So max 3 points per line.\n\nThus to cover 15 points with lines covering at most 3 points each, we need at least ceil(15/3) = 5 lines. So 5 lines is the theoretical minimum. So it's plausible that a covering exists with 5 lines. We need to find a covering that uses exactly 5 lines each covering 3 points (or some covering 2 points). The challenge is to find a set of 5 lines covering all points. Let's try to find a systematic covering.\n\nWe can think of a covering as a partition of points into at most 5 lines. Since each line can have at most 3 points, we need at least 5 lines, and we need to achieve exactly 5 lines covering all points. So we need to partition the 15 points into 5 groups of 3 points each, each group collinear with a sunny slope.\n\nWe already have two groups of 3 points: group A = {(1,3),(3,2),(5,1)} (slope -0.5). Group B = {(1,5),(2,3),(3,1)} (slope -2). That's 6 points covered. We need to partition the remaining 9 points into 3 groups of 3 points each.\n\nRemaining points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,4),(2,1),(2,2),(2,4),(3,3),(4,1),(4,2). Let's see if we can find three collinear triples among these.\n\nPotential triple: (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) (slope 1). That's a triple. So group C = {(1,1),(2,2),(3,3)}.\n\nNow remaining points: (1,2),(1,4),(2,1),(2,4),(4,1),(4,2). That's 6 points. Need two triples.\n\nPotential triple: (1,2),(2,4),(3,6) but (3,6) not in T_5. So not.\n\nPotential triple: (2,1),(4,2),(6,3) not in T_5. So only (2,1),(4,2) are two points.\n\nPotential triple: (1,4),(2,2),(3,0) not in T_5. So (1,4),(2,2) are two points, but (2,2) already used in group C. So maybe we need to adjust group C.\n\nLet's try different grouping. Maybe we can use line y = -2x + 6 covering (1,4),(2,2). That's only 2 points. But we need triples. Maybe we can find a triple with slope -2 covering (1,4),(2,2),(3,0) not in T_5. So no.\n\nMaybe we can use line y = 2x covering (1,2),(2,4). That's 2 points.\n\nMaybe we can use line y = 0.5x covering (2,1),(4,2). That's 2 points.\n\nThus we have three pairs left: (1,4) and (2,2) (but 2,2 used elsewhere), (1,2) and (2,4), (2,1) and (4,2). That's 6 points in three pairs. So we would need three lines covering each pair, but that would be 3 lines, plus the two 3-point lines and the triple (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) would be 6 lines total. So we need to combine some of these pairs into triples.\n\nMaybe we can find a triple that includes (1,4),(2,2),(3,0) not allowed. Or (1,2),(2,1),(3,0) not allowed. Or (1,2),(2,4),(3,6) not allowed. Or (2,1),(4,2),(6,3) not allowed. So maybe we cannot partition the remaining points into triples. Perhaps we need to use a line that contains 4 points? But we argued max 3. So maybe covering with 5 lines is impossible, meaning k=5 is impossible. But the solution claims it's possible. Let's verify if k=5 is indeed impossible. Let's try to prove a lower bound >5 lines for T_5. The solution's claim that k=5 is possible might be false. Let's try to find a lower bound.\n\nWe can consider the set of points with a=1: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5). These 5 points must be covered by lines. Since any line can contain at most one point with a=1 unless the line is vertical (x=1) which is forbidden (vertical lines are non-sunny). So each sunny line can contain at most one point with a=1. Because if a line contains two points with a=1, they have same x-coordinate, so the line is vertical, slope ∞, which is forbidden. So each sunny line can cover at most one of the points with a=1. So we need at least 5 sunny lines to cover the 5 points with a=1. However, we have only 5 lines total. So each line must cover exactly one of those points. That means each line must contain exactly one point from the set { (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5) }. So each line is determined by that point and some other points. So we need to assign each of the 5 lines to one of those points.\n\nNow consider the points with a=5: only (5,1). That point must be covered by a line that also contains a point with a=1 (since each line can contain at most one a=1 point). So the line covering (5,1) must also contain one of the a=1 points. Let's see which a=1 point can be collinear with (5,1) and not be vertical/horizontal/-1 slope. The line through (5,1) and (1,b) has slope (1-b)/(5-1) = (1-b)/4. For b=1, slope 0 (horizontal), forbidden. For b=2, slope (1-2)/4 = -1/4, allowed. So line through (1,2) and (5,1) has slope -1/4, which is allowed. So we could pair (5,1) with (1,2). For b=3, slope (1-3)/4 = -2/4 = -1/2, allowed. So (1,3) and (5,1) are collinear with slope -1/2, which we already have line L1. For b=4, slope (1-4)/4 = -3/4, allowed. So (1,4) and (5,1) line slope -3/4. For b=5, slope (1-5)/4 = -1, forbidden. So (1,5) cannot be paired with (5,1). So (5,1) can be paired with any of (1,2),(1,3),(1,4). So we need to assign (5,1) to one of those lines.\n\nNow consider point (4,1). It must be paired with a distinct a=1 point (since each line can have only one a=1 point). The line through (4,1) and (1,b) has slope (1-b)/3. For b=1, slope 0, forbidden. b=2 => slope (1-2)/3 = -1/3, allowed. So (1,2) and (4,1) slope -1/3. b=3 => slope (1-3)/3 = -2/3, allowed. So (1,3) and (4,1) slope -2/3. b=4 => slope (1-4)/3 = -1, forbidden. b=5 => slope (1-5)/3 = -4/3, allowed. So (1,5) and (4,1) slope -4/3. So (4,1) can be paired with (1,2),(1,3),(1,5). So we need to assign each a=1 point to a line that also covers some other points.\n\nNow consider point (3,1). It must be paired with a distinct a=1 point. The line through (3,1) and (1,b) has slope (1-b)/2. For b=1 => slope 0, forbidden. b=2 => slope (1-2)/2 = -1/2, allowed. So (1,2) and (3,1) slope -1/2 (already L1). b=3 => slope (1-3)/2 = -1, forbidde