amily of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly three sunny ones that satisfies the original condition for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n9. **Conclusion**  \n   The constructions above show that configurations exist for \\(k=0,1,3\\).  \n   Lemma\u202f1 together with the impossibility arguments of steps\u202f6 and\u202f7 shows that no configuration exists for any other non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\).  \n   Therefore the complete set of admissible values is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,3\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma\u202f1 (Verticalisation Lemma).**  \n*Let a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the two requirements of the problem and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. Then there exists another family \\(\\mathcal L'\\) with the same two requirements, the same number \\(k\\) of sunny lines, and in which every non\u2011sunny line is vertical.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions: the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nIf a non\u2011sunny line is already vertical we keep it.  \nAssume a non\u2011sunny line \\(M\\) is not vertical.  Because \\(M\\) is not vertical, for each integer abscissa \\(a\\) it contains **at most one** point of the set  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nLet \\(a_0\\) be the smallest abscissa of a point of \\(M\\cap S\\) (such a point exists because \\(M\\) meets \\(S\\) \u2013 otherwise we could delete \\(M\\) and replace it by any vertical line without affecting the covering).  \nReplace \\(M\\) by the vertical line \\(x=a_0\\).  \n\n*Why does the replacement not lose any covered point?*  \nAll points of \\(M\\cap S\\) have abscissa \\(\\ge a_0\\); the new vertical line contains the point \\((a_0,b_0)\\) that \\(M\\) originally covered, and it also contains **every** point of \\(S\\) whose abscissa equals \\(a_0\\).  Since any other line of the family