ss. For example, one line with q=2 covers odd x, another line with q=3 covers x ≡ 1 mod 3, etc. Could a combination of such lines cover all points? Possibly, but we need to check if it's possible to cover all points of T_k with k lines each having p+q=1. The solution's argument only considered slope -1/2, but we need to see if there is any configuration of k lines with p+q=1 that covers T_k for k≥4. Let's examine.\n\nWe have k lines L_i, each passing through (1,i). For each i, we can choose a slope p_i/q_i with p_i+q_i=1, i.e., p_i = 1 - q_i. So slope = (1 - q_i)/q_i = 1/q_i - 1. So slope = - (q_i - 1)/q_i = -1 + 1/q_i. So slopes are of the form -1 + 1/q_i, where q_i is a positive integer ≥2 (since q_i=1 gives p_i=0, forbidden). So each line L_i has integer points at x = 1 + q_i t, y = i + (1 - q_i) t = i + t - q_i t = i - (q_i - 1) t. So points are (1 + q_i t, i - (q_i - 1) t). For t≥0 integer, we need y positive: i - (q_i - 1) t ≥ 1 => t ≤ (i-1)/(q_i - 1). So t can be at most floor((i-1)/(q_i - 1)). So each line covers points with x = 1 + q_i t, y = i - (q_i - 1) t, for t from 0 up to that bound. So the line goes downwards (since slope negative). For each line, the x-values are spaced by q_i, starting at 1. So each line covers points with x ≡ 1 (mod q_i). So to cover all points (a,b) with a+b ≤ k+1, we need to cover all points in the triangle. Let's examine small k to see if any configuration works for k≥4.\n\nFor k=4, T_4 = points (a,b) with a,b≥1, a+b ≤ 5. Points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4); (2,1),(2,2),(2,3); (3,1),(3,2); (4,1). That's 10 points. We have 4 lines L_1,...,L_4 each passing through (1,i). We need to cover all points. Let's try to see if any assignment of q_i can cover all points. For each i, the line L_i covers points (1+q_i t, i - (q_i - 1) t) for t=0,..., floor((i-1)/(q_i - 1)). So for i=1, we have i-1=0, so t ≤ 0/(q_i - 1) = 0 => only t=0, so L_1 covers only (1,1). So L_1 cannot cover any other points. For i=2, i-1=1, so t ≤ 1/(q_i - 1). If q_i=2, 